{
 "result": "Built fibmod.py: Fibonacci mod 1e9+7 for n up to 1e18 (and beyond) as fast as possible. Reduction uses the Pisano period PER = 2000000016 (valid since 5 is a non-residue mod 1e9+7), so n is first reduced mod PER (< 2^31). Then a single fused table lookup step: two precomputed tables \u2014 table A holding (F(r), F(r+1)) mod MOD for all r in [0, 2^15) built by an addition (no multiplication), and table B holding (F(q*2^15), F(q*2^15 - 1)) mod MOD for q up to ~61035 \u2014 so one call is just a mask, a shift, and one 2x2 matrix product reduced mod MOD: fib(n) = (A1[r]*B0[q] + A0[r]*B1[q]) % MOD. Tables are built lazily on first call and cached (build cost ~0.01 s, ~94k entries). Verification: bench.py checks fib(n) against the naive double-and-add reference for n=0..4999 and for 400 random n up to 1e18 plus edge cases (1e18, PER-1, PER, PER+1, 2^31, 0, 1, 2, 10) \u2014 all pass; assert ref(PER)==0, ref(PER+1)==1, ref(10)==55. CLI: `python3 fibmod.py 10` prints 55, `python3 fibmod.py 1000000000000000000` prints 209783453. Throughput improved from the registered assay baseline 2.04e6 calls/s to ~2.9e6 calls/s (measured 2.94e6, ~+44%, well beyond the 0.9% noise), confirmed stable across repeated runs. Table-size sweep in exp2.py (L = 11..16) showed L=15 as the sweet spot for build cost vs. lookup speed.",
